     [0;90;1m│[0m [0;96mNote: A caller can branch on a constructor. It cannot branch on prose without taking the message apart again, so a String error asks every handler to treat every failure the same way. Writing the words at the point of failure also fixes them there, which is the place that knows least about who will read them. Wrap what a library hands you into a type of your own at the edge, and render that type where the failure is reported. Naming the prose does not make it a type: a synonym for String is a String, and a wrapper around one has a single constructor, which is still nothing to branch on.[0m
